ents could connect the abstract idea of equal parts to a real-life moment they care about.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"strength_name\": \"Concrete, warm modeling\",\n  \"strength_note\": \"You repeatedly used circles, rectangles and a cake story while counting parts aloud and praising students, which made the idea of fractions tangible.\",\n  \"horizon_title\": \"Add one easier task\",\n  \"horizon_note\": \"Next time, include a single, simpler visual problem (and a small harder challenge) so learners at different levels can practice correctly and independently.\",\n  \"journey_note\": \"Your one-session arc peaked at 68% — you led with clear models and steady momentum, and you kept showing up.\",\n  \"score_framing\": \"A 68% is a strong mid-journey signal: it shows clear strengths to build from, not a final judgment.\",\n  \"domain_whys\": {\n    \"high_leverage_practices\": \"This is developing because you used open prompts like \\\"کیا اس کی ڈیفینیشن کوئی بتا سکتا ہے؟\\\" to invite student thinking — it's not full marks because students were not asked to choose methods or present their own reasoning unprompted.\",\n    \"student_engagement\": \"This is strong because you kept many students speaking and counting parts aloud (for example the class counted '1، 2، 3، 4، 5' together) — it's not full marks because students only asked procedural questions (for example about tests or writing steps) rather than content questions about why fractions work.\",\n    \"teacher_subject_knowledge\": \"This is strong because you immediately corrected notation when a student mixed up 9 and 10, asking '9 ہم نے اوپر لکھنا ہے، اور 10؟' — it's not full marks because you did not present a non-routine problem that would produce sustained student struggle and deeper reasoning within this lesson.\"\n  }\n}","refusal":null,"reasoning":null,"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.encrypted","data":"gAAAAABq8tAVX3H9Gg6WIo0SH7Li-mXxq3j7ruGCw3PhXEZ2OD_Q7qbgui6jOIrTgK0oA27U9OzURqYWdRpr1Msm6EDH3xy0UDfX